        "text": "*[Bug 19142] New: Error for match.arg(&lt;non-string&gt;, ...) is not very illustrative*\n<https:\/\/bugs.r-project.org\/show_bug.cgi?id=19142>\n\n            Bug ID: 19142\n           Summary: Error for match.arg(&lt;non-string&gt;, ...) is not very\n                    illustrative\n           Product: R\n           Version: R-devel (trunk)\n          Hardware: All\n                OS: All\n            Status: NEW\n          Severity: minor\n          Priority: P5\n         Component: Language\n          Assignee: <mailto:R-core@R-project.org|R-core@R-project.org>\n          Reporter: <mailto:michaelchirico4@gmail.com|michaelchirico4@gmail.com>\n\nCreated attachment 3800\n  --&gt; <https:\/\/bugs.r-project.org\/attachment.cgi?id=3800&amp;action=edit>\nHandle wrong input type the same as any other in match.arg\n\n```\nmatch.arg(TRUE, c('a', 'b', 'c'))\n# Error in match.arg(TRUE, c(\"a\", \"b\", \"c\")) : \n#   'arg' must be NULL or a character vector\n```\n\nThis message is typically seen by a user mis-using a function with an enum-ish\nargument, so I see two problems here:\n\n 1. It implies a _vector_ is accepted (which is true only for\n`isTRUE(several.ok) || startsWith(several.ok, \"all\")`)\n 2. It suggests that _any_ character vector will do, when in fact there are\nonly 3 valid values.\n\nI think it would be better to just show the \"normal\" `match.arg()` error here:\n\n```\nmatch.arg('d', c('a', 'b', 'c'))\n# Error in match.arg(\"d\", c(\"a\", \"b\", \"c\")) : \n#   'arg' should be one of \"a\", \"b\", \"c\"\n```\n\n(also a nit: the first error uses \"must\" while the second uses \"should\")\n\n(of course, enough familiarity with Go has me also wondering if this should\nobey the want\/got approach more and report what was received in addition to\nwhat is expected: <https:\/\/pkg.go.dev\/testing)>\n\n----\n\nIn filing this bug I saw another deficiency in the case of `several.ok`:\n\n```\nmatch.arg(letters, c('a', 'b'), several.ok='all')\n# Error in match.arg(letters, c(\"a\", \"b\"), several.ok = \"all\") : \n#   'arg' should be one of \"a\", \"b\"\n```\n\nThis is also a bit misleading -- `letters` _does_ have `\"a\"` and `\"b\"`; what's\nwrong is that there are _some_ mismatched inputs. The error could reflect that;\nI have elected not to pursue that nuance in the initial patch here.\n\nI _have_ elected to retain the `NULL or` clarification in the updated error,\nwhich might not be desirable (e.g. I presume it will break a number of CRAN\npackages assuming base error messages are highly predictable:\n<https:\/\/github.com\/search?q=org%3Acran+%2F%27arg%27+should+be+one+of+%2F+path%3Atests&amp;type=code)>.",
